    <!--
      PRODUCT_SPEC PLAY-001 — what the car's player screen says when a book will not start.

      Written for someone driving: what happened, and the one thing they can do about it. The credential
      sentence names the phone deliberately, because signing in to a self-hosted server cannot be done from
      a head unit and telling a driver to try again here would be a lie.
    -->
    <string name="car_error_credentials_expired">Sign in again on your phone to keep listening</string>
    <string name="car_error_server_unreachable">Can\'t reach your server from here</string>
    <!--
      The server answered, and the answer was not the book: a stale library item, a rate limit, a server
      error. Kept apart from the unreachable sentence because a review found that one claiming the network
      had failed on a request the server had plainly received. Names no cause and promises no retry — a 404
      is not transient and a 429 is, and the status alone does not tell a driver which.
    -->
    <string name="car_error_server_cannot_deliver">Your server couldn\'t deliver this book</string>
    <!--
      A parsing, decoding or audio-track failure: the media or this device, never the network. Worded
      without a cause, because Media3's code says which band the failure is in and not why.
    -->
    <string name="car_error_file_not_playable">This book\'s audio could not be played</string>
    <!-- The label on the action beside the credential message. A verb, because it is a button. -->
    <string name="car_error_sign_in_action">Open BookWave</string>
</resources>
